<Layout title="Social media is not your website | Virgil Digital" description="Why Facebook, Instagram, TikTok and other social platforms should support, not replace, a small-business website.">
  <span class="kicker">Guide · Digital foundations</span><h1>Social media is not your <span class="accent">website.</span></h1>
  <p class="lede">Facebook, Instagram, TikTok, YouTube and LinkedIn can help people discover you. They should not be the only place your business exists online.</p>
  <h2>What social media is good at</h2><p>Social platforms are excellent for attention, updates, community, photos, videos, recommendations, and conversations. A good post can put your business in front of someone who was not looking for you five minutes earlier.</p>
  <h2>What you do not control</h2><p>Algorithms decide who sees your posts. Platform layouts change. Accounts can be locked or compromised. Important information gets buried in a feed, and a customer may have to scroll through months of posts to find your opening hours, menu, prices, or booking instructions.</p>
  <h2>What a website adds</h2><p>A website gives your business a permanent home you control. It can explain your services clearly, answer common questions, appear in search results, connect to your social profiles, and give customers one obvious next step: call, book, visit, order, or enquire.</p>
  <h2>How they work together</h2><p>Social media creates attention. Search and Google Business Profile capture people with intent. Your website organises the information and helps them decide. A restaurant can post a video on TikTok, share it on Instagram, and link every profile to a website with the menu, location, opening hours, accessibility details, and booking route.</p>
  <h2>What this means for a local business</h2><p>A barber with an active Facebook page may still lose someone searching “barber near me” if the website is outdated. A coffee shop may post excellent pictures but still make it difficult to find the menu or opening hours. A tradesperson may get views on TikTok but have no simple quote form. The problem is not social media. The problem is relying on one rented channel for every job.</p>
  <h2>The practical answer</h2><p>Keep using the platforms that suit your customers. Add a fast, clear website that owns the important information and gives every social visitor somewhere reliable to go.</p>
